@carlosparadis ,

I wanted to share an update on getting the GitHub commit comments workflow running in the `download_github_events.Rmd` notebook.

I was able to create the functions for downloading  GitHub commit comments (`github_api_project_commit_comments` and `github_parse_project_commit_comments`).

However, I ran into two issues when saving and paginating the results. I was able to resolve these issues, but I wanted your input on whether these approaches were good. 

### Issue 1: Files overwriting each other (affects all endpoints)

The existing code within the  `download_github_events.Rmd` notebook names files using the oldest and newest dates found on that page (e.g., `TrinityCore_TrinityCore_1770930760_1773965623.json`). The problem is that if two different pages contain data from the same date range, they end up with the same filename and overwrite each other. I saw this when testing both commits and commit comments endpoints for TrinityCore. Multiple pages (e.g., pages 27, 28, 29, ...), all saved to the same file.

To fix this, I added the page number to the filename so every page gets its own file:

`Before: TrinityCore_TrinityCore_1770930760_1773965623.json`
`After:  TrinityCore_TrinityCore_page_27_1770930760_1773965623.json`

### Issue 2: gh_next() getting stuck on commit comments

The existing code uses `gh_next()` to fetch the next page of results. When I checked the raw `Link` header that GitHub returns for the commit comments endpoint, it looked like it was pointing to the correct pages. However, when actually running the notebook, `gh_next()` still returned the same page repeatedly instead of advancing to the next page.

To fix it, I added a check that detects when the same page is returned twice and switches to asking GitHub for the next page by number directly (e.g. `page=2`, `page=3`), which bypasses `gh_next()` entirely for this endpoint:

```r
# Bug fix for commit comments: gh_next() can get stuck and return the same page twice
# If this happens, request the next page directly using page=N
if (!is.null(gh_response) &&
    page_is_commit_comments(previous_response) &&
    page_is_commit_comments(gh_response) &&
    !is.null(previous_response)) {
  previous_signature <- page_signature(previous_response)
  next_signature <- page_signature(gh_response)

  # If gh_next() is stuck, use the fallback
  if (!is.na(previous_signature) && !is.na(next_signature) && identical(previous_signature, next_signature)) {
    owner_repo <- extract_owner_repo_from_commit_comment_url(previous_response)
    if (!is.null(owner_repo)) {
      if (verbose) {
        message("Detected duplicate page from gh_next(); retrying commit comments with explicit page=", page_number)
      }
      # Ask for page N directly by number
      fallback_response <- try(
        {
          github_api_project_commit_comments(
            owner = owner_repo$owner,
            repo  = owner_repo$repo,
            token = token,
            page  = page_number,
            per_page = 100
          )
        },
        silent = TRUE
      )

      if (!inherits(fallback_response, "try-error")) {
        # Use the fallback page if it is empty or different from what we already have
        if (length(fallback_response) == 0 || !identical(previous_signature, page_signature(fallback_response))) {
          gh_response <- fallback_response
        } else {
          # The fallback explicit page request also returned duplicate content,
          # meaning there are no more new pages to fetch. Stop iterating.
          if (verbose) {
            message("Explicit page pagination returned duplicate content. Stop iteration.")
          }
          gh_response <- NULL
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

I also updated `github_api_project_commit_comments` in `github.R` to accept `page` and `per_page` as parameters (defaulting to 1 and 100) so the fallback call above works correctly.

I'm not sure why `gh_next()` doesn't work correctly for commit comments despite the `Link` header looking fine. Does this approach seem right to you, or do you have a better explanation for what might be causing it?

